\begin{quote}
\emph{Hypothesis (R), the rung reading.} The internal state space of an $n$-twistor composite is the spinor module of the $n$-th rung of the tower above $\Cl(2,4)$, $\Lambda^{\bullet}(W \oplus R_{n})$, on which the transported algebra acts as in Fact \claimref{rungs.imported-tower-one-qubit-per-rung}: each twistor of the composite occupies one new rung, and the constituents are the states with one rung occupied.
\end{quote}

The following three data on the conformal spinor space $\CS$ (twistor space) determine one another naturally:
\begin{enumerate}
\item an ordered pair of skew (disjoint) lines $(L_\infty, L_0)$ in $\PCS$, with a joint normalisation of scale;
\item a direct-sum splitting $\CS = \Som \oplus \Spi$ into two $2$-planes, with volume elements $N_\infty \in \Lambda^2\Som$, $N_0 \in \Lambda^2\Spi$ normalised by $\varepsilon(N_0 \wedge N_\infty) = 1$;
\item a hyperbolic plane in $V_\C$ spanned by an ordered pair of null vectors $\nInf, \nO$ with $\ip{\nO}{\nInf} = 1$.
\end{enumerate}
Under the correspondence, the mixed subspace $\Som \wedge \Spi \subset \Lambda^2\CS$ is exactly the orthogonal complement $\{\nO, \nInf\}^\perp$, of dimension four.

With $\CS = \Lambda^{\mathrm{even}} W$ as in Theorem \claimref{correspondence.fock-model-complexified-ascended-algebra}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item there is a canonical isomorphism $V_\C \cong \Lambda^2 \CS$ of $\mathrm{Spin}$-modules, under which the quadratic form corresponds to $P \mapsto \varepsilon(P \wedge P)$ for a fixed volume functional $\varepsilon$ on $\Lambda^4\CS$;
\item a vector is null exactly when its bivector is simple (decomposable), that is, represents a 2-plane of $\CS$, that is, a line of the projective spinor space $\PCS := \mathbb{P}(\CS)$ (projective twistor space);
\item consequently the completion's fibre quadric (Fact \claimref{correspondence.imported-forced-completion-fibre-quadric}), the space of null rays of $V$, double covers the real slice of the Klein quadric (a ray and its opposite give one point), whose points are the null lines of $V$ and are lines of $\PCS$.
\end{enumerate}

On the real slice, where $\varepsilon^{A'B'}$ is the conjugate of $\varepsilon^{AB}$ and $\bar\pi$ the conjugate of $\pi$ (the conjugation reverses the complex unit $I_{6}$): a single conformal spinor (twistor) carries null momentum $p_{AA'} = \pi_{A}\bar\pi_{A'}$ and is massless, and a system of two conformal spinors with momentum
\[
p_{AA'} = \pi^{1}_{A}\bar\pi^{1}_{A'} + \pi^{2}_{A}\bar\pi^{2}_{A'}
\]
has
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = p^{2} = 2\,\bigl|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}\bigr|^{2},
\end{equation}
the squared symplectic area between the two spinor parts. Aligned spinors (vanishing bracket) give a null composite, and mass grows with the misalignment.

The maximal hierarchy of three conformal spinors (the three-twistor hierarchy) $\C^{3} \otimes \C^{3}$ has automorphism algebra $\su(3) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1)$, of dimension seventeen, which is not the Standard Model's. The minimal massive one is: mass first exists at two constituents (Fact \claimref{mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-formula}), so matter's \emph{minimal massive} hierarchy is $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$, two conformal spinors of three pairs each, and its automorphisms form $(U(2) \times U(3))/U(1)$, with Lie algebra
\begin{equation}
\su(2) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1),
\end{equation}
of dimension $4 + 9 - 1 = 12 = 3 + 8 + 1$: the Standard Model Lie algebra exactly. The global group is determined by Theorem \claimref{standard-model.global-form-quotient-determined}.

The global gauge group of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite} is determined exactly. The kernel of the $(U(2) \times U(3))$-action on $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$ is $K = \{(\lambda I_{2}, \lambda^{-1} I_{3})\} \cong U(1)$, and
\begin{equation}
(U(2) \times U(3))/K \;\cong\; S(U(2) \times U(3)) \;\cong\; \bigl(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1)\bigr)/\mathbb{Z}_{6}:
\end{equation}
the framework's group is the smallest of the Standard Model's global forms, the full $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ quotient, not the naive product and not either intermediate quotient. Three consequences. (i) $S(U(2) \times U(3))$ is exactly the subgroup of $SU(5)$ preserving the splitting $\C^{5} = \C^{2} \oplus \C^{3}$, and none of the three proper covers $(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1))/\Gamma$, $\Gamma \subsetneq \mathbb{Z}_{6}$, acts faithfully through it; so the framework's global form is the one that grand unification's embedding requires, and the trace ratio $3/8$ of the section ``Unification and the desert'' refers to the same normalisation from the other side. (ii) The representations of the group are confined to the $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ charge lattice: on a representation of the covering product with colour triality $t \in \mathbb{Z}_{3}$, weak parity $\delta \in \mathbb{Z}_{2}$ (the class of the $SU(2)$ content: $1$ on a doublet, $0$ on a singlet or triplet) and abelian weight $q \in \mathbb{Z}$ (the integer such that the $U(1)$ factor $\lambda$ acts by $\lambda^{q}$), descent to the quotient holds if and only if $2t + 3\delta + q \equiv 0 \pmod 6$; writing $Y = q/6$ this is the condition that $t/3 + \delta/2 + Y$ be an integer. The content of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency} lies on it (each of its representations is built as a representation of $S(U(2) \times U(3))$), and the lattice check is printed there. (iii) A structural stance, registered: the lattice forbids representations off it, so a confirmed discovery of, for example, a fractionally charged colour singlet would refute the identification of the gauge group with the automorphism group of minimal massive compositeness, a stance that current searches test \cite{PDG2024}. The stance inherits the conditionality of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite}'s premise and is flagged at that level.

Under Hypothesis (S), let $T = v_{1}v_{2}v_{3}v_{4}v_{5}$, the product of the five creation operators, a grade-five element of the Clifford algebra of the ten. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] $T$ maps $\nu^{c}$, the empty state, to the full state and annihilates every other state, so the form $B(\psi, T\varphi)$ of Lemma \claimref{standard-model.invariant-pairing-five-modes} on the sixteen is the $\nu^{c}\nu^{c}$ entry alone: the right-handed neutrino's Majorana mass;
\item[(2)] $T$ commutes with $\su(3)$, $\su(2)_{L}$ and $Y$, and $[B - L, T] = -2T$; in the compact Pati-Salam algebra, the real span of the $21$, the subalgebra fixing $T$ is twelve-dimensional, exactly $\su(3) \oplus \su(2)_{L} \oplus \uu(1)_{Y}$;
\item[(3)] the orbit of $T$ under the $21$ spans thirty dimensions, on which $\su(2)_{L}$ acts trivially, and $T$ is the highest weight of an $\su(2)_{R}$ triplet: the orbit is the $(10,1,3)$.
\end{enumerate}
So a vev along $T$ breaks Pati-Salam to the Standard Model algebra and gives $\nu^{c}$ its Majorana mass at the same scale. It also breaks the thirteenth generator of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.under-s-composites-state-space-spinor}(3), which lies in $\su(2)_{R} \oplus \su(4)$ as $X = 2T_{3R} - \tfrac32(B - L)$.

Under $S(U(2) \times U(3))$, with $Y$ the hypercharge just defined:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Unconditional.) The pair-level generators, the cells of the tensor state space, transform as $(2, 3)$ with abelian weight $q = 1$, so $Y = 1/6$: the quark doublet's representation. A single pair has one unit of hypercharge.
\item (Under Hypothesis (S).) The states with one slot mode occupied and the pair modes in an $SU(3)$-invariant configuration are $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{0}W$, weight $3$ ($Y = +1/2$, total mode number one, odd), and $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{3}W$, weight $-3$ ($Y = -1/2$, total mode number four, even); both transform as $(2, 1)$. Without (S) neither exists in the state space, whose only row-filling states form the quadruplet $(4,1)$ of weight $+3$.
\item (Unconditional as a computation on the Fock space of a single conformal spinor, with the pair-slot factor acting alone; that Fock space is not a subspace of the tensor state space, and under (S) it is the row $j = 0$.) In the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector) $\Lambda^{\bullet}W = \Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3}$, the grade-$k$ states transform as $\Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, that is as $1, 3, \bar{3}, 1$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$, with weight $q = -2k$: $Y = 0, -1/3, -2/3, -1$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, under (S), the fermion content of the composite at the particle handedness (Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}) is
\begin{equation}
(2,3)_{1/6} \oplus (2,1)_{-1/2},
\end{equation}
quark doublet plus lepton doublet, with colour neutrality of the lepton the statement that a state with its pair modes full is an $SU(3)$ singlet: a lepton is a complete conformal spinor, one slot with the pair system full, while a quark is one slot with one pair.

Over one generation of the content of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}, quark and lepton doublets and the four singlets (the lepton under Hypothesis (S)), with $Q = T_{3} + Y$,
\begin{equation}
\frac{\sum T_{3}^{2}}{\sum Q^{2}} \;=\; \frac{2}{2 + \tfrac{10}{3}} \;=\; \frac{3}{8},
\end{equation}
and this is $\sin^{2}\theta_{W}$ at the unification scale under the hypothesis of Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-one-loop-running-trace-ratio}(b), the classic value, here over derived rather than assumed content. The value rests on (S) with the lepton doublet, which contributes $\tfrac12$ to each sum: over the unconditional content alone, the quark doublet and the four singlets, the same ratio is $9/26$. Over the fibre $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$ alone the ratio is $9/10$, unconditionally, and over the two doublets alone $3/4$, again under (S). Comparison with the observed $0.2312$ requires renormalisation-group running from the unification scale (the structural scale $M_{U}$), which Proposition \claimref{standard-model.spectrum-run} performs.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] For nondegenerate $V$ of dimension $n$, $\Cl(V, Q) \otimes_\R \C \cong \Cl_n(\C)$, the Clifford algebra of $\C^{n}$ with $Q(z) = \sum z_i^{2}$; it depends only on $n$. $\Cl_n(\C) \cong \Mat_{2^{n/2}}(\C)$ for $n$ even and $\Cl_n(\C) \cong \Mat_{2^{(n-1)/2}}(\C) \oplus \Mat_{2^{(n-1)/2}}(\C)$ for $n$ odd.
\item[(ii)] Let $n$ be odd, $V$ nondegenerate, and $\omega = e_1 \cdots e_n$ for an orthonormal basis ($Q(e_i) = \pm 1$), so that $\omega^{2} = \pm 1$. Then $\omega$ is central and invertible, $\Cl^{-} = \Cl^{+}\omega$, and
\begin{itemize}
\item if $\omega^{2} = 1$, the central idempotents $P_{\pm} = \tfrac12(1 \pm \omega)$ split $\Cl(V) = \Cl(V) P_+ \oplus \Cl(V) P_-$, and $a \mapsto a P_{\pm}$ is an isomorphism $\Cl^{+} \to \Cl(V) P_{\pm}$, so $\Cl(V) \cong \Cl^{+} \oplus \Cl^{+}$;
\item if $\omega^{2} = -1$, $a \otimes (x + \mathrm{i} y) \mapsto a(x + y\omega)$ is an isomorphism $\Cl^{+} \otimes_\R \C \to \Cl(V)$, with $\omega$ as the imaginary unit.
\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

Let $(\mathbb{V}, Q)$ be nondegenerate, with the completion $\Cl(p{+}1,q{+}1)$ and quadric $\mathcal{Q}$ of the section ``The completion: the conformal model''. Define $\iota : \mathcal{Q} \to \mathcal{Q}$ by $\iota[N] = [\,e_+ N e_+\,]$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\iota$ is well defined, total and continuous on $\mathcal{Q}$, and an involution.
\item For every $x$ with $Q(x) \neq 0$, \; $\iota[X(x)] = [X(x/Q(x))]$: on invertible vectors $\iota$ is the geometric reciprocal $\rho$.
\item For every $x_0$ with $Q(x_0) = 0$, that is, a null vector or the origin, $\iota[X(x_0)] = [\, x_0 + \tfrac12 \nInf \,]$, an ideal point (for $x_0 = 0$, the classical point at infinity $[\nInf]$), and this value is the limit of $\iota[X(x)]$ along every path of invertible $x \to x_0$. Consequently $\iota$ is the unique continuous map $\mathcal{Q} \to \mathcal{Q}$ agreeing with the lifted reciprocal $[X(x)] \mapsto [X(\rho(x))]$ on the lifted invertible locus.
\end{enumerate}
